course: everyday
lesson: 9
title: Tiredness, hunger and thirst: the body's signals
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Your body talks to you with feelings: thirst, hunger, tiredness, feeling hot or cold, and pain are all messages that it needs something.
explain: Thirst means you need water; a simple check is the colour of your wee: pale yellow means you are drinking enough, dark yellow means drink more.
explain: Hunger shows as a rumbling tummy, low energy, grumpiness and trouble paying attention; and after you eat, it takes about twenty minutes for your brain to feel full, so eating slowly helps you stop at the right time.
explain: Tiredness shows as yawning, heavy eyes, clumsiness and a short temper; children your age need about 9 to 12 hours of sleep a night.
explain: A boy in Arizona hiking on a hot day gets a headache and feels dizzy and sick; his body is warning him about the heat, so he stops in the shade, drinks water and tells his mum.
explain: A boy in Arizona played football all afternoon in the heat without drinking; he got dizzy, his head pounded and he fainted, and an adult had to cool him down and call for help.
explain: A girl in Glasgow skipped breakfast and lunch to finish a project; by the afternoon she was shaky and could not think, and she fell off her bike on the way home because she felt faint.
fact: Thirst is the body's signal that it needs water.
fact: Pale yellow wee means you are drinking enough; dark yellow wee means you need more water.
fact: On hot days and during sport you should drink water often, even before you feel thirsty.
fact: A rumbling tummy, low energy and grumpiness can be signs of hunger.
fact: It takes about twenty minutes after you start eating for your brain to feel full.
fact: Yawning, heavy eyes and clumsiness are signs of tiredness.
fact: Children of 6 to 12 years need about 9 to 12 hours of sleep a night.
fact: Shivering is your muscles making heat to warm you up.
fact: Sweat cools you down as it dries on your skin.
fact: Dizziness, headache and feeling sick on a hot day can mean the body is getting too hot.
fact: Pain is a warning that something may be wrong.
fact: Being tired makes it harder to learn, remember and stay calm.
fact: Heat exhaustion (feeling weak, dizzy and sick from too much heat) can turn into heatstroke, a medical emergency; one warning sign is that a hot person stops sweating.
fact: Going without food or water for a long time makes you shaky, slow to think and more likely to have accidents.
exercise: What does thirst tell you? | Your body needs water | Your body needs sweets | You need to go to sleep | You are getting taller
exercise: Your wee is dark yellow. What does it probably mean? | You need to drink more water | You have drunk too much water | You need a lot more sleep tonight | You ate too many green vegetables
exercise: What colour of wee shows you are drinking enough? | Pale yellow | Dark yellow | Orange | Brown
exercise: Which of these can be a sign of hunger? | A rumbling tummy and feeling grumpy | Shivering, chattering teeth and goosebumps | A sore throat | Itchy eyes
exercise: Why does eating slowly help you not overeat? | Your brain needs time to feel full | Slow food has fewer calories | Your stomach grows while you eat | Fast eating makes you hungrier for ever
exercise: Which are signs of tiredness? | Yawning, heavy eyes and clumsiness | A rumbling tummy | Sweating and red cheeks after a long run | Hiccups and sneezes
exercise: How much sleep does a child of nine need each night? | About 9 to 12 hours | About 4 to 5 hours a night | About 6 hours a night | About 15 to 18 hours
exercise: Why do you shiver when you are cold? | Your muscles are making heat | Your body is losing water | Your bones are getting cold inside | You are scared of the cold
exercise: How does sweating help you? | Sweat cools you as it dries | Sweat keeps germs away | Sweat makes your muscles stronger | Sweat gives your body water
exercise: On a hot day you feel dizzy, sick and have a headache. What should you do? | Get into shade, drink water, tell an adult | Run faster to get home sooner | Stay in the sun so your body can get used to it | Put on more clothes
exercise: When should you drink during sport on a hot day? | Often, a little at a time | Only when the game is over | Never, it gives you a stitch | Only if you start to feel faint
exercise: What is pain telling you? | Something may be wrong | Your body is getting stronger | You need to eat more sweets | Nothing at all to worry about
exercise: Why is it harder to do maths when you are very tired? | Tiredness makes it hard to think and remember | Numbers look smaller at night, when the light is dim and yellow | Tired people forget how to read | It is not harder at all
exercise: Your friend is cross and cannot concentrate before lunch. What might help? | A healthy snack or lunch | A long nap on the floor | Another hour of lessons | A cold shower and a run round the field
test: A girl in Texas plays soccer in the heat and notices her wee is dark. What should she do? | Drink more water | Drink less so she sweats less | Eat a salty snack only | Keep playing without changing anything
test: Ben eats his lunch in three minutes and still feels hungry, so he takes more. What is happening? | His brain has not yet had time to feel full | His stomach is broken | His lunch had no food in it | He needs to eat even faster to fill himself up
test: A boy in Alaska is shivering on a snowy walk. What is his body doing? | Making heat with its muscles | Cooling itself down | Getting ready to sleep in the snow | Losing its energy for ever
test: Ella yawns all day at school and keeps dropping things. What might she need? | More sleep at night | More sugar | More screen time | Less water
test: Which is true? | Sweat cools you down as it dries | Sweat warms you up | Sweating means you are ill | Only adults sweat, not children or babies
test: Why is it a bad idea to ignore pain in your foot during a long walk? | Pain warns that something may be wrong | Pain makes your foot stronger | Pain always goes away by walking faster | Pain is just a feeling with no meaning
test: Leo stays up until midnight on a school night. What will probably happen the next day? | He will find it harder to concentrate | He will learn faster | He will grow taller overnight | He will feel more awake than usual all day long
test: A girl in Perth feels dizzy and sick after hours on the beach in the sun. What do her body's signals mean? | She is too hot and needs shade and water | She needs to swim further out | She is hungry for ice cream only | Her body is just bored
test: Which shows that a child's body has enough water? | Pale yellow wee | Dark yellow wee | A dry mouth | A headache
test: my wee's dark yellow, that bad? | You need to drink more water | You drank too much water | It means you are very healthy | It means you need to sleep
test: just ate, still hungry, more? | Wait about 20 minutes to feel full | Eat as fast as you can | Hunger never goes away | Have a big fizzy drink instead to fill you up
test: why am i shivering, it's freezing | Your muscles are making heat | Your body is cooling down on purpose | Your bones are too cold | You are sweating too much
test: kid's 10, how long should he sleep | About 9 to 12 hours | About 5 hours a night | About 6 hours a night | About 15 to 18 hours
test: playing in the sun, feel dizzy and stopped sweating | Get into shade, drink water and tell an adult now | Keep playing, it will pass | Run to get your blood moving | Sit in the sun and rest
test: not hungry, gonna skip lunch and play | Eat something, you will get shaky and tired later | Skipping meals makes you stronger | Food only matters at dinner | Drink fizzy drink instead of eating
test: Leo says if u r not thirsty u don't need water on hot days. right? | No, drink often on hot days before you feel thirsty | Yes, thirst always comes first | Yes, water makes you slower | Yes, you sweat less if you do not drink
test: did u hear Ollie fainted at sports day, they're saying he didn't drink anything all day cos he wanted to win | Real lesson: drink water often on hot days and in sport; the reason may be just a rumour | Real lesson: winners never need water during sport | It proves only boys faint on sports day | It is just gossip, so water does not matter in sport
test: someone's mum said if u eat super fast ur stomach can actually explode lol | That is a blown-up rumour; eating fast can make you overeat because fullness takes about 20 minutes to feel | It is true, stomachs often burst from fast eating | It is true, so you should eat only once a day | It is true, but only with fizzy drinks
test: Chloe fell asleep in class and snored, everyone's sharing the clip | Do not share it; she may be very tired and it would embarrass her | Share it, because snoring in class is hilarious | Share it, so the teacher sees she was lazy | Share it, but blur her face a little bit
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises urine of a dark yellow colour? | It indicates that a greater quantity of water ought to be consumed | It indicates that an excessive quantity of water has been consumed | It indicates that the person has slept for too long | It indicates that the person is in excellent health
exam: Identify the quantity of nightly sleep generally recommended for children aged six to twelve years. | Approximately nine to twelve hours | Approximately four to five hours | Approximately six hours at most | Approximately sixteen to eighteen hours
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning shivering. | It is the generation of heat by the muscles for the purpose of warming the body | It is the means by which the body releases excess heat | It is a sign that the body has an excess of water | It occurs only when a person is hungry
exam: It being given that a child experiences dizziness, headache and nausea upon a hot day, determine the most probable explanation. | The body may be becoming excessively hot, which could progress to heatstroke | The child has merely eaten too much breakfast | The child has slept for too many hours the previous night | The child is simply bored and requires a game
